/* Wealth Lab · Module 5 Insurance and Risk academic course · 2026-11-02 */
window.WealthLabAcademicM5Data={"schema":"wealth-lab-m5-academic-v1","version":"3-academic-addon","reviewDate":"2026-11-02","lessonIds":["L5.S1","L5.S2"],"lessons":[{"id":"L5.S1","title":"Term vs. Permanent Life Insurance","subtitle":"Mortality risk, contracts, premiums, reserve mechanics, policy loans and estate coordination","prerequisites":["L1.S1 household assets and liabilities","L1.S3 present values and cash-flow math","L3.S2 trust rights and tax basis","L4.S2 investment risk"],"objectives":["Differentiate protection risk transfer from savings and investment","Estimate the household death-benefit need from obligations and resources","Compare guaranteed and nonguaranteed policy illustrations","Model cash-value, surrender-charge and policy-loan scenarios","Distinguish loan liquidity from free wealth or insured deposits","Explain policy ownership, beneficiary and estate coordination"],"sections":[{"title":"Risk pooling, insurable interest and the insurance balance sheet","body":["Life insurance is a contingent financial contract. Policyholders pay premiums to an insurer, and the insurer promises a benefit after a covered insured death if policy conditions are satisfied. The death benefit is not a claim the insured can spend while alive simply because it appears on the declarations page. Underwriting estimates mortality and related risk; premiums also support acquisition costs, servicing, statutory reserves and insurer capital. State law and contract wording govern the exact promise. The insured life, contract owner, premium payer and beneficiary can be different parties with different legal rights.","Insurers aggregate independent or partially correlated mortality exposures, invest premiums and maintain reserves against expected claims. An insurer's solvency cannot be established by a single glossy projection or dividend history. Regulatory supervision, financial strength, claims practices and state guaranty rules matter. Consumer protection arrangements have eligibility and monetary limits and should not be treated as an unconditional federal guarantee. The policyholder's best practical defense is to understand the legally guaranteed values and avoid premiums that cannot be sustained.","Insurance purchasing should start with the financial loss caused by death. A surviving adult may lose wages, noncash household work, health coverage access, childcare, or the ability to pay a mortgage. A child may need support for many years. Conversely, a person with no dependents, sufficient savings and no liabilities may need relatively little coverage for income replacement. A policy should match economic obligations and the time those obligations last, not a marketing rule that all people need the same face amount.","Use source-dated evidence: the insurer's filed policy form, full illustration where relevant, declarations and riders, beneficiary form and premium schedule, plus regulatory materials from the NAIC and Connecticut Insurance Department. Distinguish a general financial education example from an individual underwriting quote. The coverage amount, lapse terms, renewal choices and exclusions must be verified before drawing conclusions about a specific contract."],"worked":["Hypothetical annual premium $360 for a level term policy, with $250,000 stated death benefit.","The $250,000 is conditional protection, not present cash-value savings; assumed term cash surrender value is $0.","A beneficiary may receive $250,000 after covered death if policy remains in force and exclusions or adjustments do not apply.","Ten-year gross term-premium outlay $360×10=$3,600, disregarding time value and changes in premiums.","Compare the $3,600 premium cost with the probability-weighted protection value and household risk objective, not a guaranteed monetary investment return."],"practice":[["Why is a $250,000 term-life death benefit not added to today's bank account balance?","It is a conditional payment triggered by an insured event and contract conditions, not an unconditional current deposit or readily accessible living cash asset."],["How can a life policy be financially valuable even when no death benefit is paid during the term?","The household has purchased risk protection against a potentially catastrophic loss; insurance value is not solely the monetary return on premiums in the no-claim outcome."]]},{"title":"Determine the coverage need using a dated survivors' balance sheet","body":["The death-benefit need is a liability-and-income problem. One approach is needs analysis: identify funeral and settlement costs, debt payoff, dependents' ongoing living expenses, education support and transitional costs, then subtract liquid assets and dependable survivor benefits already available. The result is a provisional funding gap rather than an automatic policy recommendation. If a spouse could cover the mortgage and expenses on their own income, coverage may focus on the remaining gap; if an adult provides unpaid caregiving, replacing that work can also be expensive.","Discount recurring expenses when translating a stream of annual shortfalls into capital today. For an end-of-year constant annual shortfall C for n years and a model annual discount rate r, PV=C×[1−(1+r)^(-n)]/r, with PV=C×n when r=0. An inflation-sensitive stream needs consistent nominal growth and nominal discount assumptions, or an explicitly real calculation. A simple multiple-of-salary rule ignores taxes, duration, savings and dependent needs. The calculation should consider both life expectancy and the specified duration of family dependency.","For example, suppose a survivor would face a $20,000 annual net income gap for ten years, a $100,000 mortgage payoff goal, $15,000 immediate expenses and $40,000 accessible assets. At a hypothetical 4% discount rate, the ten-year end-of-year shortfall has present value about $162,218. Adding mortgage and immediate expense goals and subtracting cash suggests a coverage gap around $237,218. This is not a quote or legal assumption about loan acceleration; it is one illustration of funding adequate survivor capital.","Before recommending coverage, identify existing employer group life benefits, their portability, policy exclusions, social security survivor eligibility where applicable, insurance already held, available cash and outstanding creditors. Reassess after purchase of a home, a child's birth or adulthood, job changes and beneficiary changes. A larger face amount does not replace the need for correct beneficiary designations and an organized claims process."],"worked":["Annual survivor cash shortfall $20,000 for ten year-end periods.","Illustrative discount rate 4%; discounted gap PV≈$20,000×[1−1.04^(-10)]/0.04≈$162,218.","Other needs: $100,000 mortgage-payoff objective + $15,000 immediate costs − $40,000 liquid assets.","Modeled coverage gap ≈$237,218; actual plan may prioritize continuing mortgage payments over payoff.","A $250,000 proposed face amount exceeds the modeled gap by about $12,782 under these specific assumptions."],"practice":[["Why may income replacement be a better starting point than an automatic ten-times-salary formula?","A dated expense gap can account for actual survivors' earning capacity, dependent years, debt, liquid reserves and real financial obligations, whereas a fixed salary multiple does not."],["What would change the calculated need if a surviving adult had a reliable pension or existing life coverage?","Those dependable benefits could offset the modeled funding gap, subject to their eligibility, timing, taxes and termination conditions; the policy need should be recalculated."]]},{"title":"Term insurance contracts, renewal and conversion economics","body":["Term insurance provides protection for a specified duration, often with level premiums over an initial guaranteed period if the contract provides that structure. A 20-year level-term promise does not guarantee that the same rate applies forever or that coverage will remain affordable after the term. Renewal provisions and conversion privileges vary. Conversion may permit moving to specified permanent coverage without new evidence of insurability, but pricing and product options depend on the contract. Eligibility today does not guarantee a future life policy at an attractive price.","Term insurance often offers more initial death benefit per premium dollar than permanent coverage. That makes it useful when the primary objective is temporary income replacement during child dependency or while a mortgage is large. Its lack of savings value is not a defect if the purpose is protection. Money no longer spent on large permanent-policy premiums can be saved or invested, but the household must actually follow through for the strategy to build wealth.","Compare a hypothetical $30 monthly term policy with a $250 monthly permanent policy for the same insured and initially equal face amount. Over ten years, premiums are $3,600 and $30,000, respectively, before time-value adjustment. The $26,400 difference is not automatically profit from term, since the policies can have different guarantees, term length and survival benefits. But it is genuine cash capacity available for other objectives if the cheaper suitable contract is chosen. At $220 saved monthly for ten years, contributions alone total $26,400.","Ask about increasing premiums, guaranteed renewal age, conversion deadline, exclusions, the financial consequences of cancellation and the insured's health and employment. Group coverage at work can be valuable but may end or shrink after leaving employment. An adequate insurance plan survives realistic job transitions and a family cash-flow shock instead of relying solely on benefits tied to the current employer."],"worked":["Illustrative level term premium $30/month and permanent premium $250/month.","Over ten years term premiums $3,600 versus permanent premiums $30,000, a $26,400 cash outlay difference.","If the family directs $220 monthly to savings with no yield, it contributes $26,400 over 120 months.","At hypothetical nominal 4% annually compounded monthly, $220 ordinary end-month contributions grow to approximately $32,395.","The investment value and term premium future costs remain uncertain; do not call this a guaranteed better outcome."],"practice":[["Why is it misleading to say term insurance wastes premiums if the insured lives past the term?","Protection against a specified event was provided throughout the covered period. Premiums purchase contingent risk transfer, much like homeowners protection, not a promise of cash accumulation."],["What contract feature could be relevant if health deteriorates before a level-term policy expires?","A contractual conversion privilege may allow conversion to specified permanent coverage without new medical evidence, subject to deadlines, eligible products and pricing."]]},{"title":"Permanent contracts: guaranteed values, credits and required premiums","body":["Permanent life insurance combines a death-benefit contract with values that may accumulate under the product design. Whole life, universal life, indexed universal life and variable policies have materially different rules for premiums, cash value, credited return, insurance charges, charges for withdrawals and surrender, investment risk and coverage guarantees. A participating whole-life dividend is generally a nonguaranteed element even when some base premium and cash-value schedule is guaranteed. Indexed credits are not the same as owning an index fund, and floors and caps do not establish a guaranteed positive policy-wide return after insurance costs.","Universal-life structures deduct specified costs of insurance and administration from an account value; crediting rates can change and some benefits may have distinct guarantees requiring certain premium patterns. Cash value can decline or coverage can lapse if funding and credited returns do not meet costs. Variable policies may expose account value to securities performance. Read the guaranteed illustration separately from the current-assumption and alternative scenarios, and inspect annual statements to verify actual performance.","Present-value comparisons cannot be made from premium totals alone. A $250 premium that buys insurance and a cash balance differs economically from a $30 pure risk premium; compute premiums, cash surrender value net of charges, outstanding loans, net death benefit, coverage term and alternative investment accumulations at consistent dates. Avoid using a hypothetical illustration as proof that a contract will deliver a particular dividend or crediting rate. The NAIC model-illustration rules warn explicitly against presenting nonguaranteed elements as guaranteed.","A buyer should request the insurer's current and guaranteed policy illustration, complete product contract and riders, policy fees, surrender schedule, projected in-force performance under adverse rates, guaranteed death benefit provisions, and an explanation of what happens after missed premiums. The agent's license and insurer status should be checked with Connecticut Insurance Department records. Insurance is a complex long-term commitment and should not be treated as a stand-alone high-yield savings account."],"worked":["Model ten years of $250 monthly premiums: cash paid $30,000 before time-value adjustment.","Suppose a fictional year-10 illustration shows $21,000 cash surrender value and $250,000 death benefit, with no outstanding loan.","Surrender at that year would yield $21,000 before tax or transaction adjustments, not the full $30,000 paid.","The $9,000 premium-minus-surrender gap includes the economic cost of risk coverage and contract charges; it cannot be labeled only an investment loss.","A different illustrated nonguaranteed rate could alter cash values, future premiums and lapse projections."],"practice":[["Why is an insurer's nonguaranteed illustration not suitable as a guaranteed mortgage down-payment savings forecast?","Dividend and crediting assumptions, contract charges, premiums and coverage conditions can change. The enforceable guaranteed schedule and an adverse scenario may differ substantially."],["How does a whole-life cash surrender value differ from the death benefit amount?","Cash surrender value is an amount available on surrender under contract conditions after relevant deductions, while the death benefit is a contingent insured-death payment, often net of outstanding loans."]]},{"title":"Policy loans: borrowing against collateral without creating equity","body":["A policy loan ordinarily allows the contract owner to borrow from the insurer subject to available policy loan value, with the policy securing repayment. It is still a debt. The policy's gross cash value, available loan value, cash surrender value and net death proceeds are different quantities. Loan interest accrues under contract terms; if interest is not paid out of pocket, a balance may compound. Some whole-life contracts maintain interest or dividend credits on gross scheduled values, but crediting on the underlying asset does not make loan interest free or cancel lender claims.","Assume a hypothetical owner has $20,000 policy loan value and borrows $12,000 at an annually compounded 7% loan interest rate, without repayments. The balance after five years is $12,000×1.07^5, about $16,831. Its growth consumes more of the policy's collateral margin, regardless of whether cash values also grow under a separate schedule. A $100,000 hypothetical death benefit with unchanged face amount would be reduced by the unpaid loan and loan interest under a typical loan-offset structure, potentially leaving about $83,169 before other adjustments.","The policy's remaining loan capacity is not cash already owned in a bank. The household may spend loan proceeds for a down payment, but it now owns a secured policy loan. If the funds are transferred to purchase real estate, the household may hold more home equity or cash at closing but less net insurance collateral; there is no double wealth created by borrowing against the policy. The mortgage lender may also need to document the financing source and obligations; it is not automatically acceptable 'gift cash'.","A dangerous feedback loop occurs when loan interest capitalizes while policy value underperforms its illustration. Loan and charges may erode the collateral, cause the need for new premium funding or threaten lapse. If the owner cannot afford continuing premiums and loan costs, the strategy can collapse exactly when the family needs protection. Evaluate annual in-force illustrations with the actual loan balance, interest assumptions, cash values and lapse threshold, not only an initial quote."],"worked":["Hypothetical policy loan principal $12,000, annual rate 7%, no payment for five years.","Ending loan debt = $12,000×1.07^5≈$16,831.","Illustrative gross death benefit $100,000 less loan ≈$83,169, before policy-specific adjustments.","Even if the policy earns a credit on some value, the loan's contracted debt has not disappeared.","The $12,000 loan proceeds can be spent once; loan liability must remain on the household economic balance sheet."],"practice":[["Why is taking a policy loan to buy a house not a financial method that creates new net worth on the borrowing date?","The borrower receives cash and simultaneously incurs debt secured by policy rights. A change in how cash is invested does not eliminate loan principal or interest."],["What would you request before trusting a policy-loan illustration with five years of capitalized interest?","A current insurer-produced in-force illustration showing guaranteed and nonguaranteed policy values, actual existing loans, interest capitalization, surrender values, planned premiums and possible lapse dates."]]},{"title":"Lapse, surrender and taxation: why the exit matters","body":["Life-insurance death benefits paid by reason of death are generally excluded from federal gross income under ordinary rules, with important exceptions such as transfer-for-value arrangements. Interest paid on retained death proceeds can be taxable. Surrender of a cash-value contract can generate ordinary taxable income when proceeds exceed the owner's investment in the contract under the tax rules. Those statements are different tax events. One cannot conclude every policy transaction is tax free simply because the death benefit is usually excluded.","Outstanding policy loans can complicate surrender or lapse accounting. Suppose a contract has an adjusted investment in contract of $22,000, a cash surrender value of $30,000 before loan-offset and an unpaid loan of $18,000. A simplified full-surrender economic result could involve $12,000 net cash plus discharge or setoff of $18,000 debt, an aggregate $30,000 of value accounted for. Under a simplified taxable surrender approach, $30,000 less $22,000 basis yields $8,000 potential taxable gain. Actual tax treatment requires contract and accounting records, including prior distributions, MEC status and adjustments.","A modified endowment contract, or MEC, is a life insurance contract that meets statutory premium-funding tests triggering different distribution-tax rules. Loans or other value accessed from a MEC may be treated differently for taxation and potential additional taxes. The label 'permanent life' does not establish that a contract is or is not a MEC. Even non-MEC arrangements can create adverse tax events upon surrender or lapse with policy loans. IRS Publication 525 and competent tax review are better starting points than marketing statements about tax-free loans.","Plan the exit before paying the first premium: what if the policy is surrendered in year five, transferred into a trust, partially withdrawn, assigned to a lender, or ends with a high loan balance? Identify the contractual nonforfeiture rights, tax basis, guaranteed net surrender value, beneficiary and estate consequences, and whether premiums can be maintained during unemployment. Tax treatment and legal transfer restrictions are specific to the facts and should not be invented by a calculator."],"worked":["Hypothetical policy basis $22,000; gross surrender value $30,000; outstanding loan $18,000.","Net cash on full surrender before other charges $30,000−$18,000=$12,000.","Economic value received may include $12,000 cash plus $18,000 extinguished loan = $30,000.","Simplified potential taxable gain $30,000−$22,000=$8,000, subject to exact contract and tax characterization.","At an illustrative 22% marginal rate, $8,000 recognized ordinary taxable gain could imply $1,760 tax before other return effects."],"practice":[["Why can life-insurance surrender create taxable income even when the owner receives little net cash after an outstanding loan is settled?","Tax accounting can treat the extinguished policy loan as value in the surrender transaction. Net cash received and the amount compared with policy investment basis are not identical."],["Why must an insured confirm whether a contract is a modified endowment contract before planning loans?","MEC classification can alter the tax treatment of loans and distributions and may raise additional tax issues. The contract's funding history and statutory rules must be examined."]]},{"title":"Insurable household comparison: permanent coverage versus term plus reserves","body":["The appropriate product depends on the problem. If a family needs $250,000 of death protection for two decades and can afford $250–$500 of monthly saving, a large permanent premium could compete directly with emergency reserves and a future home purchase. Conversely, some households with long-lasting coverage needs, special beneficiaries or specific estate liquidity problems may have reasons to evaluate permanent insurance despite higher costs. It is not academically sound to universally say term is always best or permanent is a magical asset-building method.","Construct an apples-to-apples comparison with the same insured, underwriting class, face amount, required coverage horizon and credible premium assumptions. Path A pays a hypothetical $30 monthly term premium and directs $220 monthly elsewhere. Path B pays a hypothetical $250 permanent policy premium. The alternatives differ in lapse risk, liquidity, guarantees, investment exposure, surrender values and beneficiary outcomes. Explicitly note whether the term coverage expires before modeled death and whether a replacement policy is available.","At 4% nominal annual saving return compounded monthly, $220 invested at each month-end for ten years produces approximately $32,395. That is before tax and investment costs, and it could be much smaller after adverse markets. A fictional permanent policy might have a year-10 surrender value of $21,000 with a death benefit still in force. Comparing $32,395 and $21,000 alone is invalid if it ignores the insurance cost after year ten, the differences in permanent guarantees or risk-adjusted value. Use both financial statements and insurance protection scenarios.","Stress-test household affordability. If $250 premium would consume half of a $500 monthly savings capacity, ask whether a $1,200 medical bill or property repair would force policy lapse or debt. Include the opportunity cost of a late premium payment and the uncertainty of future coverage. Make the ultimate recommendation conditional on product documents, present and future insurance needs, and a sustainable full budget."],"worked":["Term premium $30/month and permanent premium $250/month, same hypothetical initial coverage face.","Monthly cash difference $220; 120 months of contributions $26,400.","At 4% nominal annual monthly compounding, future savings ≈$32,395 before fees and tax.","Compare with fictional year-10 permanent surrender value $21,000 and continuing coverage, but do not infer product superiority from these two figures alone.","If available household surplus is $400/month, a $250 permanent premium uses 62.5% of available monthly surplus."],"practice":[["What important information is missing from a comparison that only contrasts year-ten bank savings with permanent policy surrender value?","Remaining life coverage, premium obligations after year ten, guaranteed cash values, lapse risk, taxes, alternative investment risks and death-benefit differences must all be compared."],["Why is it risky to purchase a permanent policy primarily to borrow for a house soon afterward?","Early cash value can be limited, surrender or loan access may be costly, loan interest continues, and premium obligations and mortgage affordability remain separate."]]},{"title":"Policy ownership, trusts, beneficiaries and inheritance coordination","body":["A life policy has distinct roles: owner, insured, beneficiary and often contingent beneficiary. The owner generally controls permitted contract transactions; the beneficiary receives proceeds according to valid designations and policy terms after an insured event. A revocable trust may serve as beneficiary or owner under an appropriate estate plan, but title and policy rights need actual insurer forms. A life-insurance trust may have more restrictive administration and tax implications. Simply changing a beneficiary designation does not make a mortgage loan disappear.","Estate-tax inclusion depends on policy ownership, retained incidents of ownership, beneficiary arrangements and the applicable statutory rules, including potential issues with transfers close to death. A trust is not a universal exclusion mechanism. Estate taxes and the ordinary income-tax exclusion for death benefits are different regimes. An irrevocable trust may change who controls and receives proceeds, but an improperly drafted or funded arrangement can frustrate the family's objective or cause avoidable costs.","Many families have modest estates relative to 2026 federal and Connecticut estate-tax exemptions; sophisticated irrevocable life-insurance trusts are not automatically justified. Coordination can still matter for a minor beneficiary or property that must be maintained during settlement. The NAIC reminds consumers that naming a minor beneficiary may require a trust or estate-management arrangement. A trustee may legally receive proceeds but still need sufficient liquidity to cover immediate mortgage, insurance and household expenses.","An integrated inheritance plan records the policies, contract owner, beneficiary percentages, backups, claims instructions, policy loans, premiums and trust authority. A family wishing to leave a home to a daughter must also review deed, first mortgage, subordinate assistance liens and probate or trust documents. An insured's death can generate money but it does not guarantee that the property becomes debt-free without deliberate allocation of those funds."],"worked":["Hypothetical $250,000 death benefit; outstanding policy loan plus interest $16,831.","Common net benefit illustration $250,000−$16,831=$233,169 before other contract adjustments.","If two beneficiaries share equally under valid designations, each would receive $116,584.50 in this simplified example.","No portion of the mortgage principal disappears automatically merely because death benefit is payable.","A policy owned by a trust and a policy naming trust as beneficiary are distinct legal structures that require review."],"practice":[["Why can changing a policy's beneficiary to a trust fail to achieve the same estate outcome as transferring contract ownership?","Ownership rights, beneficiaries, retained control and tax inclusion can differ. Valid insurer records and legal review determine who has rights before and after death."],["What should an heir verify before treating a life benefit as cash available to pay off a home's mortgage?","Policy in-force status, covered claim, loan offset, named beneficiary, processing time, trust restrictions and whether the beneficiary has legal authority to allocate proceeds to debt."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["NAIC · Life insurance consumer guide","https://content.naic.org/consumer/life-insurance.htm"],["NAIC · Life insurance illustrations rules","https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/committee_related_documents/committees_lhatf_582.pdf"],["Connecticut Insurance Department · Life and annuities FAQs","https://portal.ct.gov/cid/consumer-information-center/life-and-annuities"],["California DOI · Policy types, loans, surrender and lapse guidance","https://www.insurance.ca.gov/01-consumers/105-type/95-guides/07-life/life-ins-guide.cfm"],["IRS Publication 525 · Life insurance income and surrender","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p525"],["Connecticut Insurance Department · Verify an insurance license","https://portal.ct.gov/cid/consumer-information-center"]],"assessment":{"title":"Insurance design and policy-loan forensic case","prompt":"A household requires 10 years of $20,000 annual survivor income shortfall, $100,000 mortgage objective, $15,000 immediate costs and $40,000 cash. Evaluate coverage need using 4% nominal annual discounting; compare term and permanent premiums; model a $12,000 policy loan accruing 7% for five years and how death benefit changes; model a taxable surrender with basis $22,000 and $18,000 loan. Write a professional memo distinguishing policy guarantees from projections, owners and beneficiaries, loan interest and tax unknowns.","rubric":["Correct death-benefit needs model (20%)","Policy type and illustration analysis (20%)","Loan, lapse and surrendered-value mathematics (25%)","Tax, ownership and beneficiary legal caveats (20%)","Evidence, documents and ethical recommendation (15%)"]}},{"id":"L5.S2","title":"Protecting a Home and a Family","subtitle":"Property, flood, disability, liability and long-term-care risk engineering","prerequisites":["L5.S1 insurance contracts and financial protection","L2.S3 housing payment anatomy","L1.S2 dated household cash flow","L4.S2 liquidity and stress testing"],"objectives":["Map covered versus excluded perils and replacement cost","Quantify deductibles, limits, coinsurance and out-of-pocket loss","Integrate flood and hazard risk into a mortgage payment","Model disability income elimination periods and survivor cash runway","Explain auto, umbrella and personal liability protection gaps","Build a household risk registry and disaster-recovery financing plan"],"sections":[{"title":"Insurance as a layered household balance-sheet defense","body":["The purpose of a household insurance program is to transfer defined low-frequency, severe financial losses while retaining manageable routine costs. Insurance cannot eliminate physical hazards, prevent injury or guarantee that a claim will be paid. Each policy specifies who is insured, the property or event covered, policy period, amount of protection, deductible, exclusions, conditions, endorsements and claims process. A homebuyer needs to understand which risks remain self-funded, because even an affordable mortgage becomes unaffordable after an uninsured disaster or prolonged disability.","Begin with a risk register listing the hazard, plausible severity, likelihood range if supportable, exposed asset, existing protection, retained deductible and next action. A roof loss can affect the house and temporary lodging; a lawsuit can affect future income and savings; a long-term disability can reduce earnings and mortgage capacity without causing any home damage. Insurance products operate under different contractual triggers; an entire program cannot be assessed by adding up face amounts alone.","The analytical difference between expected cost and catastrophic capacity is crucial. A household may face a small average expected annual fire loss, yet lack $100,000 of liquid funds to rebuild after a severe event. Premiums can be rational even when expected insurer payouts are smaller than premiums because the contract protects solvency under a low-probability, high-severity loss. But high deductibles and exclusions can make the real protected amount much less than the advertised policy limit.","State regulation and insurer forms matter. Connecticut Insurance Department offers a homeowners coverage and dispute guide, and its October 30, 2026 public advisory highlights license verification and complaint resources. The policy declarations, endorsements and exclusions are controlling sources for the family's coverage. A person should not assume an insurer must pay for every cause of damage merely because the property is listed as insured."],"worked":["Hypothetical home $250,000 value; owner holds $15,000 bank savings.","Uninsured $80,000 severe covered-type catastrophe would exceed cash by $65,000.","If valid insurance instead covers $80,000 loss with a $2,500 applicable deductible and sufficient limit, simplified reimbursement $77,500.","Household would pay $2,500 deductible, before excluded items, depreciated settlement, temporary lodging or other losses.","Premium cost should be compared to financial loss distribution and risk capacity, not simply expected claims."],"practice":[["Why can a $1,800 annual premium be economically rational even if the insured rarely files claims?","The premium purchases contingent protection against loss that could exceed the household's reserves, subject to the contract; it is not simply a prepaid refund account."],["Why is the policy's quoted maximum limit not equal to the amount received after every event?","Coverage triggers, exclusions, deductibles, sublimits, depreciation, coinsurance, actual covered damage and policy conditions can all constrain claim payments."]]},{"title":"Replacement cost, actual cash value and the rebuilding problem","body":["Market value of a residence is the price a buyer might pay for land and improvements. Replacement cost estimates the expense to rebuild the insured structure with appropriate materials, labor, codes and conditions, excluding land value. Actual cash value (ACV) typically applies an applicable depreciation concept to covered damage or uses policy/state-specific valuation rules. A $250,000 purchase price is therefore not proof that dwelling coverage of $250,000 is adequate; local labor and building-code costs can drive replacement spending above or below market value.","Read the homeowners declarations and endorsements. Coverage A addresses the dwelling; other categories can include other structures, personal belongings, loss of use and personal liability. Extended or guaranteed replacement-cost endorsements can add valuable protection, but terms and caps vary. Roof claims may be settled on different bases, and insurer underwriting may inspect roof age and condition. The Connecticut Insurance Department's February 2026 roof advisory describes how insurers use inspections and aerial imagery to evaluate risk, underscoring the need to confirm insurability before closing.","Consider a destroyed structure requiring $320,000 of reconstruction, but policy dwelling limit $250,000 without relevant extra coverage. If the full $320,000 is covered in type but the limit caps payment at $250,000 and a $2,500 deductible applies, a simple model might pay $247,500, leaving $72,500 of reconstruction cost with the household. Depending on policy application, deductible and limit sequencing can vary; the point is that market value and face limit can leave a very large funding gap.","Obtain a written replacement-cost estimate, quotes from carriers and line-item endorsement descriptions before selecting a mortgage. Separate earthquake, earth movement, flood and sewer backup hazards as the actual policy defines them. The plan should also include temporary lodging, replacement of belongings and mortgage continuity while the home cannot be occupied. A lienholder's insurance requirement protects collateral and does not guarantee the owner has sufficient personal property or loss-of-use coverage."],"worked":["Hypothetical rebuild cost $320,000, dwelling limit $250,000, deductible $2,500.","For an illustrative contractual payment structure, paid amount up to $250,000 less $2,500 = $247,500.","Rebuild gap $320,000−$247,500=$72,500 before land, inflation or excluded items.","Even with a $250,000 purchase price, insured reconstruction demand could be $320,000 under these assumptions.","A valid extended replacement-cost endorsement could alter this scenario; verify exact terms."],"practice":[["Why is a recent home purchase price an unreliable measure of required dwelling insurance?","Purchase price includes land and market conditions, while replacement protection reflects cost to reconstruct covered improvements under actual labor, materials and code rules."],["What important categories besides structural repair should be reviewed after a catastrophic covered loss?","Additional living expense, personal contents, detached structures, code upgrade, debris removal, deductibles, sublimits, loan payments and other excluded losses."]]},{"title":"The mathematics of deductibles, sublimits and claim frequency","body":["A deductible determines the portion of a covered loss borne by the policyholder before the insurer pays under applicable provisions. Some homeowners policies use a fixed-dollar deductible; others may apply percentage-based deductibles to named storm or wind/hail claims, calculated on insured value or another specified base rather than the damage amount. Named peril, cause-of-loss, policy limits and occurrence definitions determine which deductible applies. Compare the premium effect of deductible choices with the availability of cash on the date of loss.","On a $300,000 insured dwelling, a hypothetical 2% wind deductible tied to dwelling limit is $6,000. A $12,000 covered wind damage claim could therefore result in $6,000 paid by the policyholder and $6,000 of covered insurer payment, assuming no further limits or exclusions. A $2,000 fixed deductible under a different quotation would leave $10,000 covered for the insurer on the same loss. The lower-deductible option may have higher annual premiums; it is not automatically a better contract without understanding the premium difference and frequency of claims.","Expected-cost analysis can compare annual premium plus expected retained loss, but probability estimates must be sourced rather than invented as certainty. In a teaching scenario, assume a 10% annual probability of a single $8,000 covered loss and no other losses. With a $1,000 deductible, expected out-of-pocket loss is 0.10×$1,000=$100. With a $5,000 deductible, it is $500. If the higher-deductible policy saves $250 per year, total simplified annual expected cost may still be higher by $150. Yet expected cost is not the only objective when liquidity could be depleted by a $5,000 claim.","Small claims, severe events and repeated claims require separate sensitivity. The household should avoid treating an insurance policy as a maintenance fund; wear, neglect and ordinary deterioration can be excluded. Build a deductible reserve alongside emergency cash and investigate mitigation steps, insurer rate comparisons and claim reporting requirements. Document the coverage limit applied before declaring a model result."],"worked":["Dwelling limit $300,000; hypothetical 2% wind deductible = $6,000.","Covered wind loss $12,000 ⇒ simple insurer pay $6,000, household share $6,000.","Alternative $2,000 fixed deductible ⇒ insurer pay $10,000 on the same assumed covered loss.","Illustrative annual 10% probability of one $8,000 loss: expected retained deductible $100 at $1,000 versus $500 at $5,000.","If higher deductible reduces premium by only $250, expected annual cost rises by $150 in the simplified model."],"practice":[["Why should a homeowner not compute a 2% wind deductible as 2% of the particular $12,000 loss without reading the contract?","Percentage deductibles may be measured against the home's insured value or specified policy base. The 2% of $300,000 example is $6,000, not $240."],["What does the expected-cost comparison fail to reveal about a high deductible?","A large one-time expense may be impossible to fund from reserves even if expected average annual cost appears acceptable; risk capacity matters."]]},{"title":"Flood and sewer backup: a separate peril and financing decision","body":["Typical homeowners insurance excludes flooding. Flood insurance may be obtained through the National Flood Insurance Program (NFIP) or eligible private insurers; which perils and expenses are covered depends on the contract. A flood is not equivalent to every form of water damage. Water entering from a burst pipe, rain through storm damage, groundwater seepage or a sewer backup may be classified differently under the applicable forms and endorsements. A mortgage may require flood insurance for properties in specified flood zones, but purchasing outside a mapped high-risk area does not mean flood loss is impossible.","Connecticut Insurance Department explains that standard NFIP residential flood policies generally permit up to $250,000 building coverage and separately up to $100,000 personal property coverage, subject to applicable eligibility, terms and exclusions. The federal NFIP standard contract does not simply cover temporary housing, basement personal property or every outside structure. A private flood policy might differ, but do not extrapolate federal terms to private forms. Confirm property-specific FEMA maps and lender requirements, not just distance to a river.","Suppose a property suffers $90,000 of flood-damaged structural loss and $15,000 covered contents loss. With adequate flood limits and illustrative $2,000 building deductible and $1,000 contents deductible, simplistic reimbursement might be $88,000 plus $14,000, or $102,000. If the homeowner bought no flood policy and a standard homeowners contract excludes the cause, the modeled reimbursement could be zero. Real adjustment depends on loss classification, applicable actual cash value/replacement cost conditions, sublimits and coverage. None of these amounts predicts a claim result.","Model flood cost alongside property taxes and mortgage payments: annual flood premiums add cash requirements that can reduce loan qualification or household reserves; lenders may collect them in escrow. Purchase timing may matter because a new NFIP policy can have a waiting period with specific exceptions. A prudent buyer confirms a binding effective date before closing or storm season and keeps a flood evacuation and documentation plan in addition to insurance."],"worked":["Illustrative flood building loss $90,000 with $2,000 deductible; contents $15,000 with $1,000 deductible.","Assuming all damage is covered and limits are sufficient, building payment $88,000 and contents $14,000.","Total $102,000 hypothetical reimbursement, before exclusions and policy adjustment details.","Without applicable flood coverage, the typical homeowners flood exclusion may leave the entire modeled $105,000 to the household.","Annual flood premium of hypothetical $1,200 adds $100/month to housing expense, before tax or other effects."],"practice":[["Why is a standard homeowners policy not enough to protect a home from a flooded river?","Ordinary homeowners coverage typically excludes flood; covered flood requires separate applicable insurance and correct policy effective dates, perils and limits."],["Why is $250,000 in NFIP building coverage not equivalent to $250,000 for home furnishings and hotel stays?","Building and contents have separate limits and terms, and standard NFIP coverage generally excludes additional living expenses; limits do not merge into unrestricted cash."]]},{"title":"Disability and the elimination-period cash-flow bridge","body":["Life insurance protects financial dependents after insured death, but does not ordinarily replace income when the worker remains alive and cannot work. Disability insurance may provide periodic benefits under the contract's definition of covered disability, elimination period, benefit period, offsets, exclusions and claim requirements. Own-occupation and any-occupation definitions can materially change benefits. Employer short-term and long-term disability plans may cover less than full earnings, and group plans may end with employment. Claim approval and the benefit start date are not guaranteed by the presence of a payroll deduction.","An elimination period is a waiting period before contractual benefits begin, often stated in days; monthly benefits during the elimination period are not automatically paid. If a household's essential spending is $3,200 monthly and a disability eliminates $4,000 monthly earnings, a 90-day elimination period can require approximately $9,600 for three months of expenses in the simple no-other-income case. A contract paying 60% of a $4,000 monthly insured-income base might then provide $2,400 monthly gross benefit, still leaving $800 of modeled essentials unfunded each month. Actual net benefits depend on taxation and other income.","Social Security Disability Insurance has stricter eligibility than ordinary short-term income protection and generally a five-full-month waiting period before benefits begin, with exceptions described by SSA. The program typically requires a qualifying disability preventing substantial work for at least a year, not a short-term injury. It is inaccurate to treat SSDI as an immediate substitute for private disability coverage. Other household earnings, employer paid time off, state programs and savings should be mapped explicitly without assuming eligibility.","A proper planning model includes current emergency reserves, waiting period, expected benefit amount after offsets, benefit duration and ongoing unavoidable bills. A family that can cover two months of expenses may still fail during a four-month waiting period or if claims processing is delayed. Insurance should complement accessible cash, and policy language should be reviewed for conditions that might exclude particular causes of disability."],"worked":["Net essential monthly outlay $3,200 and assumed zero reliable income during a 90-day modeled waiting period.","Three months of expenses demand about $9,600 accessible reserve.","After wait, hypothetical benefit 60%×$4,000 eligible monthly income = $2,400 gross/month.","Monthly unfilled essentials gap $3,200−$2,400=$800 before tax and offsets.","One year of post-wait shortfall would be $9,600 in this simplified constant-expense scenario."],"practice":[["Why does a disability policy with a $2,400 monthly benefit not eliminate the need for emergency savings?","The elimination period, benefit limits, taxes, exclusions, claim delays and an expense level above benefits can require significant cash even when the policy ultimately pays."],["Why should a worker not assume SSDI begins after a minor injury keeps them off work for two weeks?","SSA disability eligibility generally requires a much more severe and prolonged impairment, relevant work history and a waiting period; short-term absence is not automatically covered."]]},{"title":"Personal liability, auto exposure and umbrella limits","body":["Property insurance pays for specified losses to covered property. Liability insurance responds to certain claims that the insured caused bodily injury or property damage to another party, subject to exclusions and defense provisions. Auto bodily injury and property-damage liability limits protect different risks from homeowners personal liability. An umbrella or excess liability policy may provide additional limits over underlying eligible policies, but it can require minimum underlying coverage and may not duplicate every exclusion. The advertised umbrella limit is not a guarantee that every lawsuit is covered.","Imagine an at-fault covered auto accident producing $350,000 in eligible third-party damages. If the applicable auto bodily-injury liability limit for that claim is $100,000 and no other coverage applies, the simple uncompensated amount beyond limit is $250,000, subject to legal issues. A $1 million qualifying umbrella that attaches after the underlying policy may respond for covered excess damages; verify attachment, covered conduct, insured persons and whether the insurer has a duty to defend. An umbrella is not a replacement for correct auto or homeowners base coverage.","Financial analysis of liability is about rare large judgments as well as legal expense. Assets titled in a trust are not necessarily immune from tort claims, and a revocable trust generally should not be marketed as complete creditor shelter. A homeowner operating a side business, hosting frequent gatherings, using vehicles or holding rental property may have liability exposures not fully handled by an ordinary homeowner policy. Business exclusions and separate business coverage need a policy review.","Coverage review should compare each exposure, primary policy limit, retained deductible, relevant exclusions and umbrella contract. Confirm that all household drivers and properties are correctly declared and that the policy's actual stated limit applies on a per-person, per-occurrence or aggregate basis as appropriate. State minimum required limits are legal compliance floors, not necessarily adequate financial protection for a family's wealth."],"worked":["Illustrative covered third-party damages $350,000 from an at-fault incident.","Applicable auto liability available $100,000 under stated simple scenario.","Amount beyond primary policy limit $250,000 if no other payment reduces claim.","A qualifying umbrella with adequate excess limit could cover some or all of the extra $250,000 subject to contract and insurer liability.","Without coverage, the family's assets and future income may face exposure subject to liability and collection law."],"practice":[["Why should a driver not assume a $1 million umbrella automatically covers every claim above a basic auto policy?","The umbrella has eligibility requirements, attachment points, definitions of insureds and occurrences, limits and exclusions; only covered claims qualify."],["How can a trust-held home and an umbrella policy address different legal and economic problems?","A trust governs ownership and administration; liability insurance supplies contingent defense and indemnity under policy terms. Neither is a universally complete replacement for the other."]]},{"title":"Long-term care, healthcare coverage and catastrophic family costs","body":["Long-term care risk is not identical to acute hospital bills. It can involve ongoing assistance with daily living, cognitive impairment care, assisted living, nursing facilities and care supplied by relatives. Medicare, Medicaid, private long-term-care insurance and hybrid policies have different eligibility and payment rules. Neither Medicare nor a general health insurance card guarantees unlimited custodial care. Medicaid planning includes income and asset eligibility, transfer rules and possible estate recovery; a trust alone does not guarantee qualification.","Long-term-care policies may specify daily or monthly benefit limits, elimination periods, maximum benefit pools, eligible facilities, inflation riders, shared-care options and conditions for claims such as needing help with specified activities of daily living or cognitive impairment. Premiums can be significant and may change depending on product and regulatory approval. Hybrid life/long-term-care policies can change death benefits when accelerated benefits are used. The family must read the contract to determine what is actually protected.","Construct a purely hypothetical cost exposure: a covered care scenario incurs $6,000 each month for 24 months, $144,000 total. Assume a private policy has a 90-day waiting period that in this illustration means no benefit for the first three months and then pays $4,000 per month for the remaining 21 months. Policy payments would total $84,000 and the family's simplified retained costs $60,000, before inflation, taxes, offsets, uncovered services or detailed elimination-period rules. A quoted monthly benefit is not the same as full reimbursement of care expense.","An adult supporting a child or partner should assess caregiver time and household income, not just nursing facility bills. If one adult leaves work to deliver care, wages and employer benefits may be lost. Insurance affordability and reserve goals must be evaluated alongside retirement, housing and estate intentions. A Connecticut attorney or qualified benefits professional should handle actual eligibility, Medicaid and special-needs issues."],"worked":["Illustrative care expenses $6,000/month for 24 months ⇒ $144,000 total.","Assume first three months receive no benefits: household directly pays $18,000.","Remaining 21 months: policy pays $4,000/month = $84,000; family pays $2,000/month = $42,000.","Total household modeled responsibility $60,000 and insurance benefits $84,000.","Actual elimination periods and covered-service rules can yield very different results, requiring policy interpretation."],"practice":[["Why is a long-term-care contract advertising $4,000 monthly benefits not the same as full coverage for a $6,000 monthly care bill?","A monthly cap, waiting period, excluded services, finite benefit pool and claim eligibility can leave meaningful uncovered costs even when the insurer pays."],["What is a major weakness of promising Medicaid will pay after moving a home into an irrevocable trust?","Asset eligibility, trust rules, transfer look-back periods, estate recovery, retained rights and state-specific exceptions require legal analysis; a trust name alone guarantees nothing."]]},{"title":"Mortgage protection and risk-budget integration","body":["A home-buying decision is incomplete without the cost and availability of property insurance. Mortgage principal-and-interest may be fixed, yet hazard insurance, flood premiums and deductibles can increase. An insurer can decline new coverage or renewals subject to applicable law and underwriting; an old roof or unaddressed hazard may complicate closing. The lender's mortgage insurance protects the lender or guarantor against default, not the borrower's ability to rebuild after a fire. These products should never be conflated.","Construct a complete monthly housing risk budget. In a hypothetical case principal and interest are $1,500, property tax escrow is $480, homeowners hazard insurance is $150, flood insurance is $100, mortgage insurance is $100 and regular repair savings $220. These items total $2,550, before utilities, transport and other family spending. If a renewal adds $600 per year to hazard premium, monthly housing expense grows by $50; if an escrow shortage adds temporary repayment, the cash need grows further even with a fixed loan rate.","The annual risk-budget schedule should also show deductible capacity. A $6,000 wind deductible and a $2,500 general property deductible may be triggered by different events; household reserves should contemplate the maximum likely immediate expense rather than pretending all policies use a uniform $500 deductible. Add disability waiting-period cash and liability coverage gaps, then determine which risks can coexist, such as a major storm during a job interruption.","Compare the cost of coverage, expected household liquidity and the magnitude of uninsured loss. Saving $25 per month by lowering coverage can be a poor decision if it creates a six-figure uninsured reconstruction shortfall. Yet spending premiums on every conceivable low-severity risk can crowd out essential reserves. Prioritize catastrophic insolvency risks, legally required protections, documented policy exclusions and sustainable recurring costs."],"worked":["Illustrative P&I $1,500 + tax $480 + hazard $150 + flood $100 + mortgage insurance $100 + repairs $220.","Total monthly housing risk budget = $2,550 excluding other costs.","Annual hazard premium increases by $600 ⇒ monthly expense +$50; new modeled total $2,600.","With take-home income $4,400 and nonhousing essentials $1,800, remaining margin $4,400−$2,600−$1,800=$0.","A single $2,500 covered-loss deductible cannot be funded from zero monthly cash margin without existing reserves."],"practice":[["Why does a fixed-rate mortgage not guarantee the homeowner's full monthly housing cost will stay fixed?","Property tax, property and flood insurance, mortgage insurance, maintenance and escrow shortages can change separately from fixed contractual principal and interest."],["Why is private mortgage insurance not an alternative to homeowners hazard insurance?","Mortgage insurance generally protects the lender's credit exposure on default, while hazard insurance addresses specified physical property losses; they transfer different risks."]]},{"title":"Claims, mitigation and building the household recovery file","body":["A household's financial risk plan must work during a claim, not only at purchase. Document the insurer, policy number, declarations, coverage and exclusions, insured parties, premiums, deductible, claim notice deadlines, photos, receipts, inventory and lender loss-payee instructions. Following a storm or fire, protect life safety, mitigate additional damage where feasible, keep evidence and record communications. Do not assume all emergency expenses are covered or incur expensive repairs without understanding policy conditions and emergency exceptions.","Insurance adjustment can dispute cause, scope, valuation or coverage; these are distinct issues. Connecticut Insurance Department's consumer information distinguishes an insurer's repair estimate from policy appraisal processes, and provides complaint assistance when an insurer may not be following state insurance rules. An appraisal clause may address valuation disagreements but generally does not automatically resolve a coverage dispute. Seek policy-specific and legal advice for serious claims and respect deadlines.","Build an executable '72-hour liquidity' schedule. If covered storm damage leads to $2,500 deductible, $1,200 temporary housing costs not yet reimbursed and a $500 emergency supply bill, the immediate cash required can be $4,200, even if an insurer ultimately pays most repair costs. Insurance proceeds may arrive after assessment and documentation. A family holding only $2,000 unrestricted cash would face a $2,200 immediate gap. A deductible reserve and accessible emergency fund complement policy limits.","The final course assignment asks the learner to prepare a household protection policy: keep primary insurer details, separate flood and storm limits, disability waiting periods, income-replacement resources, liability limits, long-term-care exposures and beneficiaries. The document should state open verification questions and identify which licensed agent, claims adjuster, attorney, social service or tax adviser should answer them. A clear risk inventory improves financial decisions without pretending that every unexpected event can be forecast."],"worked":["Hypothetical immediate loss cash: deductible $2,500 + temporary lodging $1,200 + emergency supplies $500.","Required near-term funds $4,200, before claim proceeds.","If accessible emergency cash is $2,000, the first-week cash gap is $2,200.","A valid insurance claim may cover more than this amount eventually, but timing and exclusions are uncertain.","Include policy contact details, deductible list, photographs, claims records and a liquid expense reserve."],"practice":[["Why can a household with a sufficient building insurance limit still run out of cash immediately after a disaster?","Deductibles, emergency lodging, repairs, cleanup and claim payment timing may require cash before reimbursement; not every incidental expense is necessarily covered."],["What separates a covered-damage valuation disagreement from a coverage dispute with an insurer?","Valuation asks how much covered damage costs; coverage asks whether the policy responds to the event at all. Policy appraisal and other remedies may address them differently."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["Connecticut Insurance Department · Homeowners and renters insurance","https://portal.ct.gov/cid/consumer-information-center/homeowner-and-renter-insurance"],["Connecticut Insurance Department · Flood insurance fact sheet","https://portal.ct.gov/cid/consumer-resource-library/flood-insurance-fact-sheet"],["NAIC · Homeowners insurance basics","https://content.naic.org/consumer/homeowners-insurance.htm"],["National Flood Insurance Program · Buy and understand coverage","https://www.floodsmart.gov/get-insured/buy-a-policy"],["SSA · Disability eligibility and waiting period","https://www.ssa.gov/benefits/disability/qualify.html"],["Connecticut Insurance Department · 2026 roof underwriting advisory","https://portal.ct.gov/cid/consumer-information-center/consumer-alerts/2026-consumer-alerts/advisory-2026-02-06"],["Connecticut Insurance Department · Insurance complaint resources","https://portal.ct.gov/cid/consumer-information-center"]],"assessment":{"title":"Connecticut household risk-engineering and insurance audit","prompt":"Build a source-dated insurance coverage matrix for a fictional $250,000 Connecticut home with $320,000 reconstruction cost, a possible $90,000 flood damage, 2%-wind deductible on $300,000 insurance, and a 90-day disability wait. Compute expected retained expenses, claim liquidity and housing budget; model a 24-month long-term-care claim and $350,000 third-party liability event. Distinguish insurer risk transfer, uncovered hazards, legally required mortgage insurance and beneficiary outcomes; deliver a practical recovery file and sources.","rubric":["Property and flood coverage versus exclusions (25%)","Deductible, replacement and cash-flow calculations (25%)","Disability, liability and LTC exposure model (25%)","Financing and coverage matrix integration (15%)","Primary evidence and claims implementation (10%)"]}}],"capstone":{"title":"Connecticut Household Insurance and Home-Ownership Risk Committee","intro":"A comprehensive fictional family protection case. Students must separate contractual guarantees, conditional policy rights, economic net worth and short-term cash requirements. This exercise is neither an insurance quote nor legal, lending or tax advice.","scenario":["A Connecticut household owns a house with estimated market value $250,000. Replacement cost for the structure is hypothetically $320,000. The dwelling policy limit is $250,000 with $2,500 general deductible, no assumed extra replacement coverage. Bank cash is $15,000.","Potential flood structural damage is $90,000, plus $15,000 contents. Assume separately purchased flood coverage with enough applicable limits and illustrative deductibles $2,000 building and $1,000 contents. Ordinary homeowners insurance excludes flood under its standard policy.","A wage earner has $4,000 monthly eligible income and essential household spending is $3,200 per month. Hypothetical private disability benefits are 60% of eligible wages, with a modeled three-month elimination period and no reliable other household income. Actual policy eligibility is unknown.","A 20-year family protection goal requires $20,000 annual net survivor support for ten years, a separate $100,000 mortgage-payoff objective, $15,000 immediate needs, and $40,000 of available survivor assets. Discount rate is a hypothetical 4% annually, with benefits required at each year end.","A permanent life policy has a hypothetical $100,000 gross face benefit, loan principal $12,000 at 7% annually accrued for five years without repayment, plus ongoing premiums and cash values not explicitly specified. The family wants to borrow for a house while continuing policy protection.","An alternative term contract costs an illustrative $30 per month versus $250 per month for permanent coverage. Comparable protection lengths and underwriting must be verified. The family can save the $220 monthly premium difference at a model 4% nominal annual rate compounded monthly for ten years.","A separate hypothetical policy surrender has gross surrender value $30,000, owner investment in contract $22,000 and $18,000 loan offset. A different possible contract could be classified a modified endowment contract, requiring separate tax treatment.","The family also faces a modeled $350,000 covered auto liability claim with $100,000 primary insurance limit, potential umbrella insurance subject to contractual attachment, and a possible $6,000 per month long-term-care need for two years.","The family hopes to transfer the house and policy to trust structures for an intended child beneficiary. They must review actual first-mortgage, subordinate-loan, policy owner and beneficiary rights and income- and estate-tax treatment with professionals."],"tasks":["Prepare a present household asset/debt schedule: do not treat death benefit as current cash and do not treat loan proceeds as net new wealth.","Calculate simplified property rebuilding out-of-pocket shortfall for $320,000 rebuilding costs, $250,000 coverage limit and $2,500 deductible. Identify contract-specific uncertainty.","Calculate flood claim payments under supplied covered-loss/deductible assumptions and contrast with the potential no-flood-policy outcome.","Build a monthly disability income bridge, including the first three months of waiting and first year of eligible benefit plus retained expenses. Differentiate private insurance from SSDI.","Compute present value of survivor needs and coverage gap with 4% discounting. State what changes if survivor income, assets, or required years change.","Model compound policy-loan interest over five years, net death proceeds and a critical lapse/surrender scenario. Distinguish debt from available policy value.","Compare ten-year term versus permanent premium cash and hypothetical $220 monthly savings future value at 4%, noting incomparable future guarantees and tax exposure.","Analyze the separate surrender case and why $12,000 net cash may coexist with greater amount realized and taxable contract gain, subject to actual contract and tax rules.","Calculate third-party liability excess after primary limits, analyze umbrella attachment and a 24-month LTC care case with partial benefits.","Write a complete protection recommendation including sources, coverage verification, claim-bridge cash, deed/mortgage and beneficiary coordination, and five stress triggers requiring reconsideration."],"answers":["Current bank cash is $15,000; house economic value $250,000. Death benefit is a contingent contractual claim, not $100,000 of unrestricted current cash. First mortgage or other home debt must be stated separately when known.","Under the specifically assumed structure, payout $250,000−$2,500=$247,500, leaving $320,000−$247,500=$72,500 rebuild gap. Real coverage, deductible and replacement adjustment conventions require policy review.","Flood building $90,000−$2,000=$88,000 and contents $15,000−$1,000=$14,000, combined $102,000 if qualifying coverage and full limits apply. Without flood coverage and with a typical exclusion, modeled insurer flood payment may be zero.","Disability waiting-period cash need ≈$3,200×3=$9,600. Afterward gross benefit 60%×$4,000=$2,400/month, leaving a simplified $800 monthly essential gap before taxes and offsets. Benefit duration and SSA restrictions differ.","Survivor support PV=$20,000×(1−1.04^(−10))/0.04≈$162,217.92; add $100,000 mortgage + $15,000 immediate need and subtract $40,000 liquid assets ⇒ modeled $237,217.92 need.","Life policy loan $12,000×1.07^5≈$16,830.62. Common illustrative gross $100,000 death benefit net after loan≈$83,169.38, subject to actual contract. This loan is not interest-free or a source of new equity.","Ten-year term premiums $30×120=$3,600, permanent $250×120=$30,000, premium difference $26,400. At 4% nominal annual, compounded monthly, investing $220 at each month-end for 120 months yields≈$32,394.96, before fees and taxes. Continuation-of-coverage differences matter.","Surrender gross value $30,000 less $18,000 loan = $12,000 cash. Simplified economic amount $30,000 including the settled loan, less $22,000 adjusted investment in contract gives $8,000 potential taxable gain, subject to IRS rules, prior transactions and MEC status.","Covered third-party liability $350,000 less $100,000 primary payment leaves $250,000 potential excess subject to law. LTC cost $6,000×24=$144,000; illustrative 3-month no-benefit wait then 21 months at $4,000 insurer benefit gives $84,000 paid and $60,000 household costs.","Risk memo should prioritize affordability, flood/roof coverage verification, deductible and waiting-period reserves, underwriting, policy-in-force and loan stress illustration, appropriate beneficiary ownership and professional tax/legal review."],"rubric":["Contract classification, guarantees and data verification (15%)","Life need, loan and surrender mathematics (25%)","Property, flood, disability and LTC numerical models (25%)","Household cash-flow and catastrophe resilience (20%)","Evidence, fiduciary duties and reasoned recommendations (15%)"],"references":[["NAIC Life Insurance","https://content.naic.org/consumer/life-insurance.htm"],["Connecticut Insurance Department · Life Insurance","https://portal.ct.gov/cid/consumer-information-center/life-and-annuities"],["IRS Publication 525","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p525"],["Connecticut Insurance Department · Flood Insurance","https://portal.ct.gov/cid/consumer-resource-library/flood-insurance-fact-sheet"],["SSA Disability Eligibility","https://www.ssa.gov/benefits/disability/qualify.html"],["Connecticut Insurance Department · Homeowners","https://portal.ct.gov/cid/consumer-information-center/homeowner-and-renter-insurance"]]},"rules":{"original_ids_unchanged":true,"original_progress_unchanged":true,"model_inputs_not_saved":true,"all_rates_illustrative":true}};
